# 0076-iop-per-instruction-fast-paths: the IOP interpreter stops doing per-instruction DMA and import bookkeeping
#
# What it fixes: the game thread of a 4-player fight spent a large share of its time in the IOP interpreter's
# per-instruction overhead: schedulePendingDma (a std::optional copy) after every instruction and every guest write,
# servicePendingDmaInterrupts / pendingDmaInterrupts.empty() once per instruction, and IopImportRegistry::decode
# fetching the instruction word that executeInstruction fetches again. Now: IopMemory::dmaStartPending() gates the
# takeDmaStart; nextDmaCycle (the earliest completion, kept by setPendingDma/refreshNextDmaCycle on every change of
# pendingDmaInterrupts) is the only thing the per-instruction check compares, and the idle-skip uses it too; step()
# fetches the word once, calls imports.decode only for `jr ra` (0x03E00008, the only word decode accepts) and hands
# the word to the new IopCpuCore::executeInstruction(cpu, word). The emulated behaviour is unchanged: same cycles,
# same interrupts in the same order at the same cycle.
# How it was verified: tools/run/perf_fight.py on the unmodified and the modified build (DON_MOVIE_SKIP_AFTER=1):
# identical state hash 7ab176a1857c3946c602461eb6117d22a5fc8337803e9558b44600f99c4ee985 (129 state lines); GameThread
# CPU ms per blank 15.8-16.4 -> 13.5 (this PC); pytest tests/regression -m slow -k fight_practice: passed.
# Based on an unfinished change by an earlier agent (work/wip-2026-10-18/iop/iop.diff), every hunk checked.
